{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And at Horeb you provoked HaShem.</b> Before he begins to open for them the exposition of the Torah in general, he reproves them for the sins that brought evil upon them and were not forgiven, and he reminds them of the matter of the spies and the waters of Meribah. Afterward he expounds the Ten Commandments and the section on the unity of the Name, and he warns them concerning the matter of idolatry with many warnings, and he warns concerning the commandments in their totality, and he says that they are all good and that through them all good shall come to them. And now he comes to begin with the specifics of the commandments, and before he mentions any one of them in particular, he goes back to reprove them and to call them to account for all their iniquities that they committed from the time they received the Torah onward. For the complaints that they made before the giving of the Torah — when they rebelled at the Sea of Reeds and complained at Marah and at Alush — he does not mention to them, because from the time they received the Torah they were all the more obligated to heed the voice of HaShem who had made a covenant with them. Therefore he opens here, \"And at Horeb you provoked,\" and he reminds them of the matter of the calf, and I have already explained it (Exodus 32:1)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:47:15.544439101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}